/-!
# `consts.py`, lines m1–m6 (Appendix B.4, Lemma 7.3(c) and (87))

* m1: `Ein(2) = ∫₀² (eʷ - 1)/w dw < 3.69` (value `3.68387…`);
* m2: `I₁ = ∫₁⁴ v (log v + 1.01) dv ≤ 14.92` (exactly `16 log 2 + 3.825`);
* m3: `I₂ = ∫₄^∞ U³ (log U + 1.01) v⁻² dv ≤ 214.8`, `U = 1 + 2 log v`;
* m4: `I₃ = 2 e^γ e^{Ein(2)} ∫₄^∞ dv/(U v²) ≤ 6.7`;
* m5: `(2.02/log 2)(I₁ + I₂ + I₃) ≤ 690`;
* m6: `690/Λ ≤ 7·10⁻⁶` for every `Λ ≥ 10⁸`.

`I₂` and `I₃` are improper integrals over `(4, ∞)`; we also prove that the integrands are
integrable there, so the bounds are not about junk values.

Method: on cells `[p, q]` of integers (`q = p + max(1, ⌊p/8⌋)`, up to `10¹³`), the integrand is
bounded by `P(U)/v²` with `P` of degree `≤ 4`, which is integrated exactly (`IntCells.lean`):
for `I₂` the tangent bound `log U ≤ log c + U/c - 1`; for `I₃` the bound
`1/U ≤ 3/c - 3U/c² + U²/c³` for `U ≥ c` (it is `(U - c)³ ≥ 0`), with `c ≤ U_p`.
-/

